  "result": "PPO is a way to train a model by trial and error. The model tries things, gets a score, and gets nudged toward what scored well, with a limit on how big each nudge can be so it doesn't break what already works.\n\nI'm skipping the derivation on purpose. Training models is out of scope for your project, and PPO needs gradients and probability first. You also don't need it to get better tool calling. You'll use an existing model, and most tool-call mistakes come from the setup around it.\n\nHere's a typical failure. A student says \"book the laser cutter Friday at 3.\" The assistant calls `book_equipment` without checking the calendar first, and the slot is taken. The fix is usually one of these:\n- a clearer tool description, like \"check availability before booking\"\n- a better loop\n- a check in code that blocks booking until the calendar was read\n\nAll of that costs far less than training, which matters with your small budget.\n\nSo the question to bring to your design review is \"how do I make the assistant call tools reliably?\" A good next step is to write the description for your `check_calendar` tool. Want to try one? If you still want the deep PPO version later, I can build that as a separate lesson after the basics.",
